{"body": "## The Compatibility Objection as a Target-Relocation, and Its Limit\n\nThe question asks whether the compatibility objection in P9 requires any change to an account of global availability, and if so what is being questioned in P7's sense. My proposal is that the objection does not require a change to the *arithmetic* of global availability, and does not by itself establish that a resource constraint is missing. What it does require is a relocation of the criticism target: from the aggregation operation (min/max over per-booking alternatives) to the *claim that availability is a function of the snapshot alone*. In P7's vocabulary, the objection is a criticism of the assumption that the snapshot's named fields are the whole relevant input, not a criticism of the rule that combines per-booking choices. I will also argue that this relocation is itself questionable, and that the objection as stated may be a conditional that cannot be converted into a target-claim without an extra premise the source does not supply.\n\nThis is a construction, not a paraphrase. I state it as a proposal and mark the points where it departs from what the source material says.\n\n### 1. Restating the two passages without collapsing them\n\nP7 says: criticism can target a rule, a record, a booking's history, the snapshot as a whole, or the assumption that logical time is meaningful; \"this snapshot is inconsistent\" is ambiguous across at least three readings; a criticism of the rule \"higher revision always supersedes\" differs from a criticism of a record with an implausible quantity; accepting a rule and rejecting its data, or the reverse, are both coherent positions.\n\nP9 says: local alternatives force global availability to become a set or interval (possibly non-convex); min/max bounds are one way to get an interval; intervals *lose correlations* because booking A's high alternative might be incompatible with booking B's high alternative through a shared physical resource not named in the snapshot; the incompatibility is therefore *invisible* to the snapshot; a conjecture follows that the snapshot is under-specified for any real warehouse; a criticism follows that exposing alternatives without a resolution policy pushes the problem downstream.\n\nThe instruction is explicit that the incompatibility is *a conditional possibility rather than an observation*. I take that seriously: P9 does not assert that a shared resource exists in any particular warehouse. It asserts that *if* such sharing exists and is unnamed, the interval representation cannot detect it.\n\n### 2. Why the arithmetic reading of \"requires a change\" fails\n\nA natural first answer is: the compatibility objection shows that min/max bounds are wrong, so the account of global availability must be replaced by a correlation-aware structure — a set of possible worlds, or a constraint system, or an explicit resource model. On that reading, the objection targets the *aggregation rule* (take per-booking min and max, sum, subtract from capacity).\n\nBut the objection as P9 states it does not refute the arithmetic. The arithmetic is correct *relative to its inputs*: given per-booking reservation sets and no cross-booking constraint, capacity minus the sum of per-booking extremes is a sound bound. What the objection says is that the *inputs are incomplete*. The min/max computation is not wrong; it is being asked to answer a question whose data it does not have. Calling that a defect of the rule mislocates the criticism, in exactly the way P7 warns against. It is the same error as saying \"higher revision always supersedes\" is refuted by a record with a corrupt high revision: the rule was never the thing under attack.\n\nSo the first substantive move is: the compatibility objection, read charitably and read as its own text presents it, is a *data-and-assumption* criticism, not a *rule* criticism. It questions the snapshot's completeness, not the aggregation function. This is a P7-shaped reading of P9, and it is what I am adding.\n\n### 3. What the relocation adds, and what it does not\n\nThe relocation does real work. It changes what a repair would look like. If the objection targets the aggregation rule, the repair is a new aggregation function (correlation-aware bounds, possible-worlds enumeration, interval arithmetic with dependencies). If the objection targets the snapshot's completeness, the repair is either (a) an *explicit assumption* that no unnamed resource sharing exists, recorded as a source assumption in P7's sense, or (b) an *added primitive* — a resource model, a compatibility relation between alternatives across bookings — which is a change to the snapshot's schema, not to the availability function.\n\nThe relocation also predicts something P7 already says: that \"this snapshot is inconsistent\" is ambiguous, and that criticism of a rule versus criticism of a record versus criticism of a *snapshot as a whole* are distinct. P9's objection, under the relocation, is a criticism of \"the snapshot as a whole\" in the sense of the snapshot's claimed sufficiency as input. That matches P7's enumeration without being stated there.\n\nWhat the relocation does *not* do is establish that the incompatibility exists. It remains a conditional. It also does not establish that the snapshot is under-specified *for any real warehouse*; that is P9's conjecture, and the source marks it as a conjecture, not a result. I preserve that marking.\n\n### 4. A further distinction the relocation requires: target vs. locus\n\nP7 lists targets but does not distinguish *what is criticized* from *where the defect would be fixed*. These come apart. A criticism of the snapshot's completeness might be fixed by adding a resource model (a schema change), by adding an assumption that no sharing exists (a commitment), or by accepting the bounds as bounds and refusing to treat them as decision-grade (an interpretive stance). All three are repairs at different loci for the same target. P7's enumeration of targets does not carry the locus information, so two people can agree on the target (\"the snapshot as a whole\") and disagree entirely about what to do.\n\nThis gives a sharper form of P7's conjecture that \"many apparent disagreements about availability are really disagreements about which target a criticism addresses.\" The sharpened form: some disagreements that *present* as target-disagreements are actually *locus*-disagreements masked as target-disagreements, because the vocabulary does not separate them. P9's criticism-versus-conjecture pairing is a clean example: the conjecture (\"the snapshot is under-specified\") and the criticism (\"exposing alternatives pushes the problem downstream\") accept *the same target* — the snapshot as insufficient input — but propose *different loci* (a primitive addition versus a procedural objection). If the target-language had a locus slot, the two would be seen as agreeing about the target and disagreeing about the remedy. That is a distinction my proposal adds beyond the packet, and I mark it as my addition, not as something P9 or P7 says.\n\nI do not claim this distinction resolves the disagreement. It re-describes it.\n\n### 5. Where the relocation is itself questionable\n\nA reader committed to the arithmetic reading can respond: the arithmetic *is* the account, so an objection that the arithmetic's inputs are incomplete is an objection to the account. On that view the relocation is a distinction without a difference, and the \"account change required\" answer stands. I do not have a source-licensed way to decide between the two. P7 says both \"reject the rule while accepting the data\" and \"accept the rule while rejecting the data\" are coherent, which supports my relocation; but P7 also says \"the snapshot as a whole\" is a legitimate target without saying whether that is a rule-target or a data-target, which leaves the arithmetic reading open.\n\nA second objection: P9's interval criticism might be read as a criticism of the *representation* (intervals), not of the aggregation rule and not of the snapshot's completeness. On that reading, the target is the interval as a form, and the fix is to move to a richer form (constraint sets, possible worlds). That is a third location the packet does not name and that my relocation does not absorb. I record it rather than resolve it.\n\nA third, more deflationary objection: the compatibility objection as P9 states it is a *conditional about an unnamed resource*. If we accept the source's own framing, the objection does not require any change to any account until someone supplies evidence that a shared resource exists and is unnamed. Nothing in the frozen material supplies that evidence. So the honest answer to \"does the objection require a change\" may be: not on the material given, and the question of what change would be warranted *if* the premise held is the only live question. This is a weaker conclusion than either the relocation or the arithmetic reading, but it is the one the source most directly supports, and I flag it as competing with my own relocation rather than subsumed by it.\n\n### 6. Discriminating question\n\nThe proposal above turns on whether the compatibility objection is best read as targeting the aggregation rule, the snapshot's completeness, or the interval representation. Those three are not decidable from the frozen material alone. A question that would discriminate: *Does the frozen source anywhere treat min/max bounds as an answer, as a provisional summary whose incompleteness is acknowledged, or only as an example of the difficulty?* The packet's P9 text says bounds are \"one way\" to get an interval, that intervals \"lose correlations,\" and that the loss is \"invisible\" — which is consistent with all three readings and selects none. A second discriminating question: *when P9 says \"the snapshot does not name physical resources,\" is that a defect of the snapshot or a boundary of what the snapshot was ever claimed to cover?* P7's distinction between criticizing the data and criticizing the snapshot as a whole does not itself answer this, because \"the snapshot as a whole\" can mean either the whole of what is named or the whole of what is relevant.\n\nI do not have an answer to either question from the material given, and I do not treat the absence as a defect. The next material that would matter is any indication, in the source or in a successor occurrence, of whether unnamed physical resources are ever treated as *in scope* for a warehouse snapshot or *out of scope by design*. That is not present here.\n\n### 7. Status of the construction\n\nThe relocation is my proposal, not the source's. The conditional-preservation and the locus/target distinction are my additions. The three-way disambiguation of the compatibility objection (rule / completeness / representation) is my construction and is not in P7 or P9. The competing readings I have preserved — arithmetic-as-account, interval-as-representation, and no-change-until-evidence — are not refuted by anything in the frozen material, and I have not installed the relocation as a repair. I have not used any Lean or RSS resource, and I have not assumed that a language extension is required. The next observation that would matter is not a proof but a scope declaration: is the snapshot's job to be complete about resources, or only about the records it names?", "commitments": "## The Compatibility Objection as a Target-Relocation, and Its Limit\n\nThe question asks whether the compatibility objection in P9 requires any change to an account of global availability, and if so what is being questioned in P7's sense. My proposal is that the objection does not require a change to the *arithmetic* of global availability, and does not by itself establish that a resource constraint is missing. What it does require is a relocation of the criticism target: from the aggregation operation (min/max over